meta {
  name: transformation - sms sender substitution (Spain)
  type: http
  seq: 5
}

post {
  url: {{host}}/notifications/api/v1/future/orders
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

body:json {
  {
    "idempotencyId": "{{random_idempotency_id}}",
    "sendersReference": "{{random_idempotency_id}}",
    "recipient": {
      "recipientSms": {
        "phoneNumber": "+34600000000",
        "smsSettings": {
          "sender": "TestSender",
          "body": "(TEST) Sender substitution test for Spain (Env.: {{Environment}} - Ref: {{random_idempotency_id}})"
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

docs {
  Verifies that ISmsSenderSubstitutionService replaces an alphanumeric sender ID with a
  numeric sender for recipients in countries (e.g. Spain, +34) where alphanumeric SenderIDs
  are not supported.

  The recipient phone number "+34600000000" is a placeholder Spanish number used only to
  match the "34" country code prefix rule; it does not need to be a real, deliverable number
  for the substitution logic (applied when the SMS is picked up for publishing) to run.

  The sender "TestSender" is alphanumeric (not a valid mobile number), so it is a candidate
  for substitution.

  Required local configuration for the substitution to actually occur:
  Add a rule to SmsSenderSubstitution:Rules (e.g. in appsettings.Development.json) mapping
  the "34" country code prefix to a numeric sender for the service owner (creator short name)
  your token authenticates as, for example:

  ```
  "SmsSenderSubstitution": {
    "Rules": [
      {
        "CountryCodePrefix": "34",
        "NumericSenderByServiceOwner": {
          "ttd": "+4791234567"
        }
      }
    ]
  }
  ```

  Without this configuration (the default is an empty Rules list), HasRules is false and the
  sender is sent unchanged, i.e. no substitution takes place.
}
